WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:05.879
Speaker 1: Ukrainian president Volodymyr Zelensky
made a sort of last minute, at least as far

00:00:05.879 --> 00:00:10.580
as it was announced, a visit to Washington,
D.C. yesterday, which included an address

00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:15.809
to Congress, a an opportunity that not many
foreign leaders get.

00:00:15.809 --> 00:00:18.020
And we will talk about that in a little bit.

00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:23.840
The speech that Zelensky delivered to Congress
about the help that the U.S. has been providing

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:30.340
to Ukraine in its defensive war due to the
invasion of Ukraine by Russia.

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:33.090
And Vladimir Putin was a good speech.

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:34.660
It was a powerful speech.

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:40.360
And also the entire event had a pretty weird
feeling to it.

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:43.600
And I think I know what the weirdness was.

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:46.750
And I'm going to talk about that in a moment.

00:00:46.750 --> 00:00:52.960
Much fanfare and we're going to get to all
aspects of this, but a big standing ovation

00:00:52.960 --> 00:01:04.690
to kick it all off.

00:01:04.690 --> 00:01:10.950
And what we're watching here, if you're watching
today rather than listening, is Zelensky entering?

00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:16.380
Too much applause and a standing ovation and
the greetings of so many different people.

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:17.880
There's Senator Patrick Leahy.

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:24.440
And you see Charles Schumer, the senator,
and all sorts of different people, Zelensky

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:26.630
wearing his typical green.

00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:32.480
I don't I don't know that I want to editorialize
the shade of green, but it is not the best

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:33.480
shade.

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:34.480
Not that we really care about that.

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:36.750
It's just a weird, colorful voice in the public.

00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:41.070
And here he greets Vice President Kamala Harris
and then the speaker of the House, outgoing

00:01:41.070 --> 00:01:42.930
speaker of the House, Nancy Pelosi.

00:01:42.930 --> 00:01:49.390
And he delivers a speech and many applause
lines in the speech, including reminding the

00:01:49.390 --> 00:01:54.330
world and the United States that Ukraine is
not out of this thing against.

00:01:54.330 --> 00:01:57.840
Speaker 2: Against all odds.

00:01:57.840 --> 00:01:59.640
And doom and gloom.

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:00.860
Scenarios.

00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:04.250
Ukraine didn't fall.

00:02:04.250 --> 00:02:08.340
Ukraine is alive and kicking.

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:15.379
Speaker 1: And another standing ovation there.

00:02:15.379 --> 00:02:22.020
And of course, it is great that Ukraine has
been able to resist what some characterized

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:27.640
and handicapped as an easy win for Russia
when this started months and months and closing

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:29.819
in on a year ago.

00:02:29.819 --> 00:02:31.970
That is an absolutely great thing.

00:02:31.970 --> 00:02:39.019
We now get into the part that I believe was
the element of weirdness, which is that there

00:02:39.019 --> 00:02:45.200
was a feeling of this, that it was sort of,
okay, we're going to give you the money.

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:49.150
New aid package, around 2 billion, including
Patriot missiles.

00:02:49.150 --> 00:02:50.739
We're going to do it.

00:02:50.739 --> 00:02:56.390
But you've got to come here and you've got
to really thank us and you've got to act extremely

00:02:56.390 --> 00:02:59.060
solicitous towards us.

00:02:59.060 --> 00:03:02.140
And that was absolutely a big element of this
speech.

00:03:02.140 --> 00:03:07.670
Speaker 2: I'm glad to read that President
Biden supported our peace initiative today.

00:03:07.670 --> 00:03:14.260
Each of you, ladies and gentlemen, can assist
in the implementation to ensure that America's

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:20.269
leadership remains solid.

00:03:20.269 --> 00:03:22.530
Bicameral and bipartisan.

00:03:22.530 --> 00:03:23.630
Thank you.

00:03:23.630 --> 00:03:28.870
Speaker 1: And, of course, it is not actually
completely bipartisan.

00:03:28.870 --> 00:03:31.790
And that's part of what's interesting about
this.

00:03:31.790 --> 00:03:36.180
There are people there are Republicans who
are sort of.

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:40.150
Soft supporters of the U.S. helping Ukraine.

00:03:40.150 --> 00:03:46.840
And then there are people like Matt Gaetz
and Lauren Boebert who are flat out just against

00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:48.930
American help of Ukraine.

00:03:48.930 --> 00:03:53.959
And they sped into the House chamber at the
last minute and sat there looking visibly

00:03:53.959 --> 00:04:01.450
bored, never clapping as a deliberate show
of how against this they are Zelensky assuring

00:04:01.450 --> 00:04:06.910
and reminding everyone in the chamber that
the money we are sending to Ukraine is not

00:04:06.910 --> 00:04:08.420
charity.

00:04:08.420 --> 00:04:13.290
It is part of the goal, shared goal of global
security and democracy.

00:04:13.290 --> 00:04:18.200
Speaker 2: Financial financial assistance
is also critically important.

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:20.209
And I would like to thank you.

00:04:20.209 --> 00:04:22.070
Thank you very much.

00:04:22.070 --> 00:04:28.320
Thank you for both financial packages you
have already provided us with and the ones

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:34.380
you may be willing to decide on your money
is not charity, right?

00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:42.290
Is an investment in the global security and
democracy that we handle in the most responsible

00:04:42.290 --> 00:04:57.430
way.

00:04:57.430 --> 00:04:58.430
Financial financials.

00:04:58.430 --> 00:04:59.800
Speaker 1: Okay.

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:08.860
And this ultimately led to a Ukrainian flag
actually being unfurled on the floor of the

00:05:08.860 --> 00:05:09.860
House.

00:05:09.860 --> 00:05:12.730
Speaker 2: Let this flag stay with you.

00:05:12.730 --> 00:05:19.910
Ladies and gentlemen, this flag is a symbol
of our victory in this war.

00:05:19.910 --> 00:05:25.160
We stand, we fight, and we will win because
we are united.

00:05:25.160 --> 00:05:31.090
Ukraine, America and the entire free world.

00:05:31.090 --> 00:05:38.919
Speaker 1: Okay.

00:05:38.919 --> 00:05:46.100
So now what's happening is Zelensky is unfurling
a Ukrainian flag, which is now being held

00:05:46.100 --> 00:05:52.800
up by Nancy Pelosi and Vice President Kamala
Harris.

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:57.600
And then similarly, you might see off to the
right of the screen that there is an American

00:05:57.600 --> 00:05:59.850
flag in a triangle shape.

00:05:59.850 --> 00:06:10.919
And then that was handed to Zelensky and off
he went with his American flag.

00:06:10.919 --> 00:06:15.819
And now this is the end of the speech and
we see Zelinski leaving with his American

00:06:15.819 --> 00:06:18.060
flag that was handed to him.

00:06:18.060 --> 00:06:21.700
So, listen, my feelings on this are very mixed.

00:06:21.700 --> 00:06:23.630
Of course, I support Ukraine.

00:06:23.630 --> 00:06:29.020
And of course, I believe that it is in the
interests of the United States and our allies

00:06:29.020 --> 00:06:33.570
to beat back the invasion by Russia.

00:06:33.570 --> 00:06:34.770
Putin's a lunatic authoritarian.

00:06:34.770 --> 00:06:38.010
I mean, listen, I've got a decade of commentary
on this.

00:06:38.010 --> 00:06:40.310
There is absolutely no question there.

00:06:40.310 --> 00:06:48.470
This entire visit, extraordinarily heavy on
the theatrics, which I feel repelled by the

00:06:48.470 --> 00:06:53.840
theatrics to a great degree because it is
such a stage managed event.

00:06:53.840 --> 00:07:00.240
But public opinion, particularly global public
public opinion depends on exactly that stage

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:01.240
management.

00:07:01.240 --> 00:07:07.440
And so while I personally find it offputting
as someone who follows this stuff all the

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:13.240
time, I have to be open to the idea that for
people who are busy working in fields that

00:07:13.240 --> 00:07:19.440
are not political media, their opinion about
what should happen may actually be shaped

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:20.599
by events like this.

00:07:20.599 --> 00:07:23.759
And maybe they want to see Zelinski come and
grovel a little bit.

00:07:23.759 --> 00:07:29.819
Maybe that's what they need in order to be
okay with or to continue seeing support of

00:07:29.819 --> 00:07:31.830
Ukraine as a worthy cause.

00:07:31.830 --> 00:07:38.620
Now, the history of speeches like this to
Congress during conflicts by world leaders

00:07:38.620 --> 00:07:42.949
of other countries, it's not the longest list
in the world.

00:07:42.949 --> 00:07:48.840
And we know, of course, of Winston Churchill's
address to Congress during World War Two as

00:07:48.840 --> 00:07:51.330
the Prime Minister of the UK.

00:07:51.330 --> 00:07:56.240
We know about Lech Walesa's address to Congress
during the Cold War.

00:07:56.240 --> 00:07:58.409
As President of Poland.

00:07:58.409 --> 00:08:02.840
We know about Nelson Mandela's address to
Congress during the apartheid era.

00:08:02.840 --> 00:08:06.110
Mandela, the first president of South Africa.

00:08:06.110 --> 00:08:13.360
And we have I may be missing a couple, but
we have Havel's address to Congress during

00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:19.080
the Velvet Revolution, the last the last and
final president of Czechoslovakia.

00:08:19.080 --> 00:08:21.370
How does this compare?

00:08:21.370 --> 00:08:28.229
How does the situation in Ukraine compare
not only on the facts, but in terms of its

00:08:28.229 --> 00:08:30.900
relation to the United States?

00:08:30.900 --> 00:08:32.750
I don't know that I have that answer.

00:08:32.750 --> 00:08:38.680
And it may require, looking back from some
future point, to really fully understand that.

00:08:38.680 --> 00:08:46.709
But a powerful speech, heavy on theatrics
that I think was sort of determined to be

00:08:46.709 --> 00:08:55.350
necessary to continue justifying to some degree
in the court of public opinion the support

00:08:55.350 --> 00:09:00.980
to Ukraine that the United States continues
to give support, which I widely am in favor

00:09:00.980 --> 00:09:07.660
of, while recognizing that those are dollars
for what is effectively speaking a proxy war

00:09:07.660 --> 00:09:11.589
against Russia doesn't make it bad.

00:09:11.589 --> 00:09:14.070
In fact, it's the right side.

00:09:14.070 --> 00:09:17.829
This was a war of aggression by Russia, but
much more complicated than any simple explanation

